<HelpLayout title="Badger on your watch" description="Log sets, run your rest timer and finish a workout from a Wear OS watch, with Badger on your Android phone.">
  <h1>Badger on your watch</h1>

  <p>On a Wear OS watch, Badger is a remote for the workout running on your Android phone. Start the workout on the phone, then log sets and run your rest from your wrist. The watch stores nothing.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="set-up">Install it</h2>
  <p>You need Wear OS 3 or newer, paired with an Android phone that has Badger. Install Badger on the phone first. Then open the Play Store on the watch, search for <strong>Badger</strong> and install it.</p>

  <h2 id="start">Start a workout</h2>
  <p>Start the workout on your phone, then open Badger on the watch or tap its tile. While the workout runs, a chip on the watch face shows your current set or the rest.</p>

  <h2 id="log-a-set">Log a set</h2>
  <p>The watch shows your next set, filled in from the plan. Tap <strong>Log set</strong>. When the phone confirms it, the watch shows <strong>Logged</strong>.</p>
  <p>To change the set first:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Turn the crown or rotary bezel to change the weight. It moves by that exercise's own step on the phone, from zero up to about twice the set's weight.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>-</strong> or <strong>+</strong> to change the reps.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Logging from the watch works wherever a set is waiting to be ticked: routine days, <a href="/help/logging/#logging-mode">Plan, then tick</a>, and any time the workout is open on the phone's training screen. In <strong>Log as you go</strong>, the watch shows the current exercise and the rest, and you add sets on the phone.</p>

  <h2 id="rest">Run your rest</h2>
  <p>The rest countdown runs on the watch, with <strong>-15 s</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> and <strong>+15 s</strong>, the same buttons as the <a href="/help/logging/#lock-screen">lock screen</a>. While the watch app is running, the phone's own rest alert stays quiet.</p>
  <p>In the last 10 seconds the countdown turns amber. The watch buzzes once when the rest ends, at the same moment as the phone. After the last set of an exercise, the line under the countdown names the next exercise, such as "Next: Hip Adduction".</p>
  <p>While the screen is dimmed, the time reads <strong>-:--</strong>, because the watch redraws too rarely for a countdown. Raise your wrist to see the time left.</p>

  <h2 id="effort">Rate the effort of your last set</h2>
  <p>During a rest, turn the crown or rotary bezel to rate the set you just finished. A small label shows the value as you turn, and the watch saves it about a second after you stop. The line under the countdown then reads, for example, "Set 2 · RIR 1".</p>
  <p>The values run from <strong>Not rated</strong> through the numbers to <strong>To failure</strong>, and the watch clicks once at each end. Not rated saves no effort for that set. The watch uses the scale the phone shows for that exercise, <a href="/help/logging/#rpe-rir">RIR or RPE</a>, and you change the scale on the phone.</p>
  <p>The line names the effort once you have logged effort on a set at least once. On a watch with no crown or bezel, tap the line and use <strong>-</strong> and <strong>+</strong>.</p>

  <h2 id="plates">See the plates for your next set</h2>
  <p>Swipe down on the set screen or during a rest. The watch draws one end of the bar with the plates for your next set, numbered from the inside out. A machine with no bar is drawn with a peg.</p>
  <p>The line under the drawing says what to change from your last set, such as "Add 35 + 25" or "45 off, 35 on". If nothing changes, there is no line. If your gym's plates can't make the weight, it says what they come to and how far off that is.</p>
  <p>The page follows the <a href="/help/tools/#plate-calculator">plate calculator</a> on your phone: the same plates, the same bar or starting weight, and the same choice of <strong>From last set</strong> or <strong>From empty</strong>.</p>
  <p>Swipe up to go back. If the phone has no plate calculator for the exercise, swiping down does nothing. When a rest ends while you are looking at plates, the watch buzzes and stays there.</p>

  <h2 id="exercises">Move between exercises</h2>
  <p>Swipe up from the set screen or during a rest to see your exercises with their done and total sets. Tap one to jump to it. Supersets advance in the same order as on the phone.</p>
  <p>Timed sets show the countdown or stopwatch with <strong>Stop</strong>, which fills in the duration as it does on the <a href="/help/logging/#set-timer">phone</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="finish">Finish the workout</h2>
  <p>After the last planned set, the watch shows the <strong>Plan done</strong> card with <strong>Not yet</strong> and <strong>Finish workout</strong>. It follows your <a href="/help/settings/#after-last-planned-set">After the last planned set</a> setting.</p>
  <p>If some sets are not ticked, the watch says <strong>Finish on your phone</strong>, because the phone asks what to do with them. After you finish, the watch shows a short summary. The full summary is on the phone.</p>

  <h2 id="no-phone">When the phone can't be reached</h2>
  <p>If the watch can't reach the phone, nothing is logged. The watch tells you and offers <strong>Try again</strong>. Log the set again once the two are connected.</p>

  <h2 id="music">Music controls</h2>
  <p>Swipe left from the workout screens to a music page that controls the music playing on your phone. It is off by default, and it works on Android phones only.</p>
  <p>To turn it on:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>On the phone, open Badger and turn on <strong>Music controls on your watch</strong>.</li>
    <li>Read the explanation and tap <strong>Continue</strong>.</li>
    <li>On Android's Notification access screen, allow Badger.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The music page shows the song, the artist and the app playing it, with <strong>previous</strong>, <strong>play/pause</strong> and <strong>next</strong>. If nothing is playing, it says "Nothing playing on your phone". Swipe right to go back to the workout. Until you turn it on, the watch has no music page.</p>
  <p>To turn it off, switch <strong>Music controls on your watch</strong> off on the phone. That stops it and removes the music page. To remove the access completely, turn Badger off in Android's Notification access screen.</p>
  <p>Android's wording says this lets an app read notifications. Badger reads only the song, artist and play state of the music session, sends them to your watch, and never stores or shares them.</p>

  <h2 id="heart-rate">Heart rate</h2>
  <p>Badger's watch app doesn't read heart rate. Run your watch's own exercise app alongside Badger for that. The phone reads heart rate per set from Health Connect: see <a href="/help/body/#heart-rate">Heart rate on your sets</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="reference">What to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Needs:</strong> Wear OS 3 or newer, paired with an Android phone that has Badger.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight unit:</strong> follows the phone's logging unit for that exercise, kg or lb.</li>
    <li><strong>On the phone only:</strong> starting a workout, adding or swapping exercises, notes, the effort scale and changing what the plate calculator starts from.</li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>
